   "etapa": "explicar",
   "piezas": [
    {
     "tipo": "explicacion",
     "titulo": "What people are like",
     "texto": [
      "The verb **ser** is used to describe what someone is like on the outside and on the inside.\n\n- Pablo **es alto** y **moreno**.\n- Ana **es alta** y **simpática**.\n- Lucía **es joven** e **inteligente**.\n\nMany adjectives end in **-o** for masculine and **-a** for feminine: alto / alta, bajo / baja, simpático / simpática. Adjectives ending in **-e** or a consonant do not change for gender: un chico **inteligente**, una chica **inteligente**; un hombre **joven**, una mujer **joven**.\n\nTo qualify the description, we place words before the adjective:\n\n- **muy** simpática (very)\n- **bastante** alta (quite / rather)\n- **un poco** baja (a little / a bit)"
     ],
     "tutora": ""
    },
    {
     "tipo": "gramatica",
     "titulo": "The adjective agrees with the person",
     "texto": [
      "In Spanish, adjectives agree in gender (masculine or feminine) and number (singular or plural) with the person or thing they describe:\n\n- Pablo is **alto**; Ana is **alta**.\n- Pablo and Juan are **altos**; Ana and Lucía are **altas**.\n- If the group is mixed, use masculine plural: ellos son **altos**.\n\nAdjectives such as **inteligente** and **joven** change only in plural: inteligentes, jóvenes."
     ],
